//! The UART drivers' protocol.
//!
//! A request is one message: byte 0 the op, then the payload. A reply's byte 0
//! is a status, then its payload.
//!
//! ```text
//! op        payload               reply payload
//! Read   1  -                     the next input byte
//! Write  2  0 to MAX_PAYLOAD      -
//!           bytes to write
//! Sync   3  -                     -
//! Mark   4  -                     -
//! Pending 5 -                     the count of input bytes buffered
//! Stats  6  -                     eight counters, `UartStats`
//! ```
//!
//! A reply that is not `Ok` carries the status byte alone, except `Lost` and
//! `Garbled`, which carry four counters (`UartCounts`).
//!
//! Nothing here is kernel surface. A device protocol belongs to the device's
//! driver and its clients; the kernel supplies the connection, the message and
//! the one-shot reply pair, equally to every protocol. The package has no
//! dependency, so `blade test` holds the wire's bytes on the host. A body is
//! `BODY_BYTES`, the kernel's `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`: a send's array type refuses
//! a skew between the two.
//!
//! # The client
//!
//! `Uart<T>` makes each request and decodes its reply into a `Result`: a
//! `Read` answers the byte, every other op `Void`, and a refusal, a reply no
//! request can produce, or the transport's own failure is a `UartError`. It
//! reaches the driver through a `UartTransport`, so a client is written once
//! and runs over whatever carries the bytes: the pipe transport in a process
//! (`drivers/uartpipe`), a fake one in this package's tests.
//!
//! # Statuses
//!
//! They are checked in this order, and the first that applies is answered:
//! `BadOp` (a message of no bytes, an op byte that names no op, or a payload
//! on an op that takes none), then `Unsupported` (an op this driver's device
//! cannot do), then `Busy` (a `Read` the driver would hold, when it holds as
//! many as it will). Every driver serves `Write`, `Sync`, `Mark` and `Stats`;
//! a driver whose device has no input answers every `Read` and `Pending`
//! `Unsupported`, at once.
//!
//! `Lost` and `Garbled` answer a `Read` only, and mark a place in the input.
//! A driver that lost input bytes, because its buffer was full or its device
//! overran, or that saw input damaged (a parity error, a frame error, a
//! break), keeps one mark at the position of the first such event. The reader
//! gets every byte before the mark, then one `Read` answered with the mark,
//! then the bytes after it. The mark consumes no byte. `Lost` is the mark
//! when any byte was lost; `Garbled` when none was and an error was seen,
//! whose damaged byte is delivered, among those before the mark. Both carry
//! the same counters: bytes lost ("at least"), frame errors, parity errors
//! and breaks, each a little-endian `u32`, counted since the previous mark.
//! While a mark is unreported the driver drops every byte that arrives and
//! counts it into the mark, so every loss sits at the one position the mark
//! names and the bytes after it arrived whole and contiguous. A driver whose
//! device cannot lose or damage input never answers either.
//!
//! # The ops
//!
//!   - `Read`  - a call. A driver whose device has input answers `Ok` and the
//!               next input byte. With none pending it holds the obligation
//!               and answers when its device delivers one; a fused `send`
//!               parks meanwhile, so a blocking read is composed entirely out
//!               of shipped parts. A read past the most it holds is answered
//!               `Busy` at once, and the client may ask again.
//!   - `Write` - a tell: post, drop the claim, never look back. The driver
//!               answers `Ok` once the payload is queued, which goes nowhere
//!               for a claim that was dropped. Queued is not sent: a driver
//!               with a transmit buffer answers before the bytes reach the
//!               device, and one without answers after. A client that calls
//!               instead learns that its bytes are accepted, and `Sync` is
//!               how it learns they are out. Every payload length, none
//!               included, is a write, so a `Write` is never refused.
//!   - `Sync`  - a call answered `Ok` once every byte written before it has
//!               left the device's transmit holding register or FIFO. The
//!               last of them may still be in the device's shift register,
//!               on its way to the wire. A connection's ring is FIFO, so the
//!               answer also proves every message posted before it has been
//!               served; that is how a client that only tells learns when its
//!               writes reached the device.
//!   - `Mark`  - a call answered `Ok`, and pure instrumentation: it tells the
//!               driver to take a trap reading, so the serve loop's cost can be
//!               measured over a window the case controls. It carries no
//!               authority and reads no device.
//!
//!   - `Pending` - a call answered `Ok` and the count of input bytes the
//!               driver has buffered, which no `Read` has taken: at most the
//!               driver's buffer, so one byte holds it. It reads no device and
//!               takes nothing. A client that reads alone waits on it for a
//!               byte to be buffered before it asks for one, instead of on a
//!               clock; each call is one round trip, answered at once.
//!   - `Stats` - a call answered `Ok` and the counters of `UartStats`, each a
//!               little-endian `u32` that holds at its top: bytes received,
//!               bytes lost, frame errors, parity errors, breaks, bytes
//!               queued for transmit, bytes sent, writes held. A driver
//!               counts what its device does and answers zero for what its
//!               device cannot do. It reads no device and takes nothing.
//!
//! A refusal is a reply, so a malformed request that was posted and its claim
//! dropped is refused to nobody: the client that wants to know calls.
//!
//! # Cancellation is not an op
//!
//! There is no `Cancel` byte. A client that gives up a `Read` drops its claim
//! (§2.1), and the driver learns of it through the request's abandoned level,
//! which it already watches because it holds the obligation. A cancel on the
//! wire would make the driver correlate two messages and would race the reply
//! it is trying to cancel; the reference count does not.

/// The bytes of a message body: the kernel's `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`.
public static BODY_BYTES: Int = 128

/// The op byte. Raw-backed because it crosses a wire: each value is stated,
/// so a reorder cannot renumber them.
public enum UartOp: UInt8 {
    case Read = 1,
    case Write = 2,
    case Sync = 3,
    case Mark = 4,
    case Pending = 5,
    case Stats = 6,
}

extension UartOp {
    /// What a transcript should call this op.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Read -> "read",
            case Write -> "write",
            case Sync -> "sync",
            case Mark -> "mark",
            case Pending -> "pending",
            case Stats -> "stats",
        }
    }

    /// Whether a request of this op carries a payload: only a `Write` does.
    public func takes_payload(&self) -> Bool {
        match self {
            case Write -> true,
            case _ -> false,
        }
    }
}

/// A reply's status byte. `Ok` is 0.
public enum UartStatus: UInt8 {
    case Ok = 0,
    case BadOp = 1,
    case Unsupported = 2,
    case Busy = 3,
    /// A `Read`'s mark: input bytes were lost at this position.
    case Lost = 4,
    /// A `Read`'s mark: none were lost, and input was damaged.
    case Garbled = 5,
}

extension UartStatus {
    /// What a transcript should call this status.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Ok -> "Ok",
            case BadOp -> "BadOp",
            case Unsupported -> "Unsupported",
            case Busy -> "Busy",
            case Lost -> "Lost",
            case Garbled -> "Garbled",
        }
    }
}

/// The counters a `Lost` or `Garbled` mark carries.
public struct UartCounts {
    /// Bytes lost, at least: a device overrun is counted as one.
    public lost: UInt32,
    public frame: UInt32,
    public parity: UInt32,
    public breaks: UInt32,
}

@synthesize
extension UartCounts: Equatable {}

/// The bytes of a mark's counters.
public static COUNTS_BYTES: Int = 16

/// What a `Stats` answers.
public struct UartStats {
    public received: UInt32,
    public lost: UInt32,
    public frame_errors: UInt32,
    public parity_errors: UInt32,
    public breaks: UInt32,
    public queued: UInt32,
    public sent: UInt32,
    public writes_held: UInt32,
}

@synthesize
extension UartStats: Equatable {}

extension UartStats {
    /// Every counter zero.
    public static func zero() -> UartStats {
        UartStats(received: 0, lost: 0, frame_errors: 0, parity_errors: 0,
                  breaks: 0, queued: 0, sent: 0, writes_held: 0)
    }
}

/// The bytes of a `Stats` answer's counters.
public static STATS_BYTES: Int = 32

/// Where the op byte sits, and where the payload starts.
public static OP_BYTE: Int = 0
public static PAYLOAD_BYTE: Int = 1

/// The most payload one message can carry: the body minus the op byte.
public static MAX_PAYLOAD: Int = BODY_BYTES - 1

static_assert(MAX_PAYLOAD > 0, "a message must have room for an op and a byte")

/// What a non-ASCII scalar becomes in `UartBody.text`: the kernel console's
/// substitute, so a line written through a driver reads the same as one
/// written through a System op.
public static ASCII_SUBSTITUTE: UInt8 = 0x3F

/// A message body, for a request or a reply. The body every send takes is a
/// `&[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]`; `&body.bytes` is what goes to the wire.
public struct UartBody {
    public bytes: [UInt8; BODY_BYTES],
}

extension UartBody {
    /// A zeroed body.
    public static func empty() -> UartBody {
        UartBody(bytes: [0; BODY_BYTES])
    }

    // The request builders answer the message's `len`.

    /// An op and nothing else.
    public func bare(&var self, op: UartOp) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        1
    }

    /// An op and one payload byte.
    public func one(&var self, op: UartOp, b: UInt8) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        self.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE] = b
        2
    }

    /// An op and a line of ASCII.
    ///
    /// A scalar that is not ASCII becomes `ASCII_SUBSTITUTE`. Text longer than
    /// `MAX_PAYLOAD` is cut, not wrapped: a silently split message would be two
    /// requests where the caller wrote one.
    public func text(&var self, op: UartOp, s: String) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op as UInt8
        var n: Int = 0
        for scalar in s.scalars() {
            if n < MAX_PAYLOAD {
                self.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE + n] = ascii_or_substitute(scalar)
                n = n + 1
            }
        }
        (n + 1) as UInt
    }

    /// A request with an op byte of any value and `payload` bytes after it,
    /// for the refusals a client tests. `payload` is at most `MAX_PAYLOAD`: a
    /// longer message is past the kernel's body, and the kernel ends a process
    /// that sends one.
    public func raw(&var self, op: UInt8, payload: UInt) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[OP_BYTE] = op
        payload + 1
    }

    // The reply builders answer the reply's `len`.

    /// A reply of the status alone.
    public func refuse(&var self, status: UartStatus) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[0] = status as UInt8
        1
    }

    /// An `Ok` and nothing else: what `Write`, `Sync` and `Mark` answer.
    public func ok(&var self) -> UInt {
        self.refuse(UartStatus.Ok)
    }

    /// An `Ok` and one byte: a `Read`'s answer.
    public func ok_byte(&var self, b: UInt8) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[0] = UartStatus.Ok as UInt8
        self.bytes[1] = b
        2
    }

    /// An `Ok` and a `Stats`' counters.
    public func ok_stats(&var self, s: &UartStats) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[0] = UartStatus.Ok as UInt8
        self.put_word(1, s.received)
        self.put_word(5, s.lost)
        self.put_word(9, s.frame_errors)
        self.put_word(13, s.parity_errors)
        self.put_word(17, s.breaks)
        self.put_word(21, s.queued)
        self.put_word(25, s.sent)
        self.put_word(29, s.writes_held)
        (1 + STATS_BYTES) as UInt
    }

    /// A mark: `status`, which is `Lost` or `Garbled`, and its counters.
    public func mark(&var self, status: UartStatus, c: &UartCounts) -> UInt {
        self.bytes[0] = status as UInt8
        self.put_word(1, c.lost)
        self.put_word(5, c.frame)
        self.put_word(9, c.parity)
        self.put_word(13, c.breaks)
        (1 + COUNTS_BYTES) as UInt
    }

    /// `v` little-endian at `at`.
    func put_word(&var self, at: Int, v: UInt32) {
        self.bytes[at] = (v & 0xFF) as UInt8
        self.bytes[at + 1] = ((v >> 8) & 0xFF) as UInt8
        self.bytes[at + 2] = ((v >> 16) & 0xFF) as UInt8
        self.bytes[at + 3] = ((v >> 24) & 0xFF) as UInt8
    }
}

/// `count + by`, held at the type's top rather than wrapping or trapping: a
/// count a client can drive must not be able to end the driver.
public func count_up(count: UInt32, by: UInt32) -> UInt32 {
    if count > 0xFFFF_FFFF - by { 0xFFFF_FFFF } else { count + by }
}

/// How many input bytes a driver buffers.
public static INPUT_BYTES: Int = 16

/// Bytes a driver's device has handed over that no client has asked for yet.
///
/// A driver buffers because a device does not wait. Draining the receiver in
/// one go turns a burst into one wake, but the bytes then have to live
/// somewhere until a `Read` asks for them, and this ring is that place. A full
/// ring drops and says so in `dropped` rather than silently: an overrun is a
/// fact about the workload, and hiding it would make a lost byte look like a
/// bug in the pipe.
///
/// The ring is here, beside the wire, so the host tests hold what a driver
/// does with a byte its reply could not deliver (`unpush`) without a device.
public struct InBuf {
    public bytes: [UInt8; INPUT_BYTES],
    public head: Int,
    public count: Int,
    public dropped: Int,
    /// Bytes a reply that found nobody home put back at the front.
    public returned: Int,
}

extension InBuf {
    public static func empty() -> InBuf {
        InBuf(bytes: [0; INPUT_BYTES], head: 0, count: 0, dropped: 0,
              returned: 0)
    }

    public func has(&self) -> Bool { self.count > 0 }

    public func push(&var self, b: UInt8) {
        if self.count >= INPUT_BYTES {
            self.dropped = self.dropped + 1
            return
        }
        var at = self.head + self.count
        if at >= INPUT_BYTES { at = at - INPUT_BYTES }
        self.bytes[at] = b
        self.count = self.count + 1
    }

    /// Put a byte back at the front: what a reply that found nobody home does,
    /// so the next reader gets the byte rather than the machine losing it.
    public func unpush(&var self, b: UInt8) {
        if self.count >= INPUT_BYTES {
            self.dropped = self.dropped + 1
            return
        }
        var at = self.head - 1
        if at < 0 { at = INPUT_BYTES - 1 }
        self.bytes[at] = b
        self.head = at
        self.count = self.count + 1
        self.returned = self.returned + 1
    }

    /// The oldest byte, or zero when there is none. The caller asks `has()`
    /// first; a server must not be endable by its own bookkeeping.
    public func pop(&var self) -> UInt8 {
        if self.count == 0 { return 0 }
        let b = self.bytes[self.head]
        var next = self.head + 1
        if next >= INPUT_BYTES { next = 0 }
        self.head = next
        self.count = self.count - 1
        b
    }
}

/// A scalar as a byte of a line: itself when it is ASCII, the substitute
/// otherwise.
public func ascii_or_substitute(scalar: Scalar) -> UInt8 {
    let v = scalar.value()
    if v < 0 || v > 0x7F {
        ASCII_SUBSTITUTE
    } else {
        v as UInt8
    }
}

/// A received message's length and bytes: all the decoders read, so they are
/// plain data and the wire is tested without a kernel.
public struct Frame {
    public len: UInt,
    public bytes: [UInt8; BODY_BYTES],
}

extension Frame {
    /// The op a received request names, or `None` for a byte that names no op.
    /// The Optional is a real domain value: a request arrives from a process
    /// the driver does not trust, so "this byte is not one of my ops" is data
    /// at a protocol boundary, never a trap. A message of no bytes names no op
    /// either, whatever its buffer holds.
    public func op(&self) -> UartOp? {
        if self.len == 0 { return None }
        UartOp.from(raw: self.bytes[OP_BYTE])
    }

    /// The op of a request the protocol accepts, or `None` for one a driver
    /// answers `BadOp`: one that names no op, or carries a payload on an op
    /// that takes none.
    public func request(&self) -> UartOp? {
        guard let op = self.op() else { return None }
        if self.payload_len() != 0 && not op.takes_payload() {
            return None
        }
        op
    }

    /// How many payload bytes a received request carries.
    public func payload_len(&self) -> UInt {
        if self.len == 0 { return 0 }
        self.len - 1
    }

    /// Payload byte `i` of a received request. Out of range answers zero
    /// rather than panicking: the sender chose the length and the receiver is
    /// a server, which a client's arithmetic must not be able to end.
    public func payload_byte(&self, i: Int) -> UInt8 {
        if i < 0 || (i as UInt) >= self.payload_len() { return 0 }
        self.bytes[PAYLOAD_BYTE + i]
    }

    /// The status a received reply carries, or `None` for a status byte no
    /// status has or a reply with no bytes.
    public func status(&self) -> UartStatus? {
        if self.len == 0 { return None }
        UartStatus.from(raw: self.bytes[0])
    }

    /// Byte `at` of a received reply; zero past its end.
    public func byte(&self, at: Int) -> UInt8 {
        if at < 0 || (at as UInt) >= self.len { return 0 }
        self.bytes[at]
    }

    /// The little-endian `u32` at byte `at` of a received reply; the bytes
    /// past its end read zero.
    public func word(&self, at: Int) -> UInt32 {
        let b0 = self.byte(at) as UInt32
        let b1 = self.byte(at + 1) as UInt32
        let b2 = self.byte(at + 2) as UInt32
        let b3 = self.byte(at + 3) as UInt32
        b0 | (b1 << 8) | (b2 << 16) | (b3 << 24)
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Replies, decoded
// =============================================================================

/// Why a request did not do what it asked.
public enum UartError {
    /// The driver refused the request: the protocol's `BadOp`.
    case BadOp,
    /// The driver's device cannot do the op: the protocol's `Unsupported`.
    case Unsupported,
    /// The driver holds as many reads as it will: the protocol's `Busy`. The
    /// read may be asked again.
    case Busy,
    /// A `Read`'s mark: input bytes were lost at this position.
    case Lost(counts: UartCounts),
    /// A `Read`'s mark: input was damaged at this position, and none lost.
    case Garbled(counts: UartCounts),
    /// A reply no request of this op can produce: a status byte that is none,
    /// or a length the status does not have.
    case Malformed,
    /// The driver is gone: its end of the connection is closed, or it
    /// dropped the request unanswered.
    case Gone,
    /// The transport failed for a reason of its own, which `code` names in
    /// the transport's terms.
    case Transport(code: UInt32),
}

@synthesize
extension UartError: Equatable {}

extension UartError {
    /// What a transcript should call this error.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case BadOp -> "BadOp",
            case Unsupported -> "Unsupported",
            case Busy -> "Busy",
            case Lost(_) -> "input lost",
            case Garbled(_) -> "input garbled",
            case Malformed -> "a malformed reply",
            case Gone -> "the driver is gone",
            case Transport(_) -> "the transport failed",
        }
    }
}

extension UartError: Printable {
    /// The description, and a transport failure's code after it. Written out
    /// of `append` calls, which allocate nothing a freestanding process lacks.
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
        match self {
            case Transport(code) -> {
                try! into.append(": code ")
                try! into.append(value: code as UInt)
            },
            case _ -> {},
        }
    }
}

extension UartError: Error {}

/// The error a refusal's status names, or `None` for `Ok`, which refuses
/// nothing.
func refusal(status: UartStatus) -> UartError? {
    match status {
        case Ok -> None,
        case BadOp -> UartError.BadOp,
        case Unsupported -> UartError.Unsupported,
        case Busy -> UartError.Busy,
        // Only a `Read` can be marked, and its decoder looks before it asks.
        case Lost -> UartError.Malformed,
        case Garbled -> UartError.Malformed,
    }
}

/// The counters a mark's reply carries.
func counts_of(reply: &Frame) -> UartCounts {
    UartCounts(lost: reply.word(1), frame: reply.word(5),
               parity: reply.word(9), breaks: reply.word(13))
}

/// A `Read`'s reply: `Ok` and one byte, a mark and its counters, or a
/// refusal alone.
public func read_reply(reply: &Frame) -> Result<UInt8, UartError> {
    guard let status = reply.status() else { return UartError.Malformed }
    if status == UartStatus.Lost || status == UartStatus.Garbled {
        if reply.len != (1 + COUNTS_BYTES) as UInt { return UartError.Malformed }
        if status == UartStatus.Lost {
            return UartError.Lost(counts_of(&reply))
        }
        return UartError.Garbled(counts_of(&reply))
    }
    if let refused = refusal(status) {
        if reply.len != 1 { return UartError.Malformed }
        return refused
    }
    if reply.len != 2 { return UartError.Malformed }
    return reply.byte(1)
}

/// A `Pending`'s reply: `Ok` and the count as one byte, or a refusal alone.
/// A driver buffers at most `INPUT_BYTES`, which one byte holds.
public func pending_reply(reply: &Frame) -> Result<Int, UartError> {
    if let status = reply.status() {
        if status == UartStatus.Lost || status == UartStatus.Garbled {
            return UartError.Malformed
        }
    }
    let count = try read_reply(&reply)
    count as Int
}

/// The reply to a `Write`, `Sync` or `Mark`: the status alone, `Ok` or a
/// refusal.
public func empty_reply(reply: &Frame) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
    guard let status = reply.status() else { return UartError.Malformed }
    if reply.len != 1 { return UartError.Malformed }
    if let refused = refusal(status) { return refused }
    return
}

/// A `Stats`' reply: `Ok` and the counters, or a refusal alone.
public func stats_reply(reply: &Frame) -> Result<UartStats, UartError> {
    guard let status = reply.status() else { return UartError.Malformed }
    if let refused = refusal(status) {
        if reply.len != 1 { return UartError.Malformed }
        return refused
    }
    if reply.len != (1 + STATS_BYTES) as UInt { return UartError.Malformed }
    return UartStats(received: reply.word(1), lost: reply.word(5),
                     frame_errors: reply.word(9), parity_errors: reply.word(13),
                     breaks: reply.word(17), queued: reply.word(21),
                     sent: reply.word(25), writes_held: reply.word(29))
}

// =============================================================================
// The client
// =============================================================================

/// What carries a client's requests to the driver and its replies back.
public trait UartTransport {
    /// Sends the first `len` bytes of `body` and answers the reply. A driver
    /// that is gone is `UartError.Gone`; any other failure of the transport's
    /// own is `UartError.Transport`.
    func call(&var self, body: &[UInt8; BODY_BYTES], len: UInt) -> Result<Frame, UartError>

    /// Sends the first `len` bytes of `body` and owes nothing back: the reply,
    /// if the driver makes one, goes nowhere.
    ///
    /// A transport carries only so many tells the driver has not yet taken
    /// (the pipe's ring holds `PIPE_INFLIGHT`), and a pipe's post needs a
    /// handle for its claim. Past either limit, a tell waits for the driver,
    /// as a call does, rather than failing: a burst of any length is
    /// back-pressure, never an error.
    func tell(&var self, body: &[UInt8; BODY_BYTES], len: UInt) -> Result<Void, UartError>
}

/// A `uartproto` client over the transport `T`.
public struct Uart<T: UartTransport> {
    /// The transport, reachable for what the protocol's ops do not cover.
    public transport: T,
    body: UartBody,
}

extension Uart<T: UartTransport> {
    /// A client over `transport`.
    public static func over(transport: T) -> Uart<T> {
        Uart(transport: move transport, body: UartBody.empty())
    }

    /// The next input byte. A driver whose device has input parks the call
    /// until a byte exists.
    public func read(&var self) -> Result<UInt8, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.bare(UartOp.Read)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        read_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// Writes `text` as one `Write`, a tell: it is out when a later `sync`
    /// answers. Text past `MAX_PAYLOAD` is cut, as `UartBody.text` cuts it.
    public func write(&var self, text: String) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.text(UartOp.Write, text)
        self.transport.tell(&self.body.bytes, n)
    }

    /// Writes one byte as a `Write`, a tell.
    public func write_byte(&var self, b: UInt8) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.one(UartOp.Write, b)
        self.transport.tell(&self.body.bytes, n)
    }

    /// Writes `text` as one `Write` sent as a call: it is out when this
    /// answers.
    public func write_now(&var self, text: String) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.text(UartOp.Write, text)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        empty_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// Answers once every request sent before it has been served.
    public func sync(&var self) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.bare(UartOp.Sync)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        empty_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// How many input bytes the driver holds that no read has taken. A
    /// driver is the only one that fills the count, and only a read empties
    /// it, so a client that is the only reader sees it only grow.
    public func pending(&var self) -> Result<Int, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.bare(UartOp.Pending)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        pending_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// The driver's counters.
    public func stats(&var self) -> Result<UartStats, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.bare(UartOp.Stats)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        stats_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// Asks the driver to take its trap reading.
    public func mark(&var self) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.bare(UartOp.Mark)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        empty_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// A call of the op byte `op` and `payload` bytes after it, whatever they
    /// name, decoded as an empty reply: for the refusals a client tests.
    /// `payload` is at most `MAX_PAYLOAD`, as `UartBody.raw` says.
    public func raw(&var self, op: UInt8, payload: UInt) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let n = self.body.raw(op, payload)
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, n)
        empty_reply(&reply)
    }

    /// A call of no bytes at all, decoded as an empty reply.
    public func nothing(&var self) -> Result<Void, UartError> {
        let reply = try self.transport.call(&self.body.bytes, 0)
        empty_reply(&reply)
    }
}
